					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/buttfranklin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2721810); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2721810&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606920802" alt="Buttfranklin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2721810)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/buttfranklin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2721810); return false;" >Buttfranklin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1510427546 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">11 Nov 2017 19:12</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3630635">
									

<p>This dialogue is absolutely fantastic. There are some downsides to the piece – I feel the exposition-by-log only outlines this idea without filling in the potentially lovely details at the core, for instance – but this dialogue more than makes up for any gripes I have. The level of realism in the speech here is beyond gold-standard.</p>
<p>I'm gushing, and I'm going to provide some unnecessary backstory here, but I'm caught up in my enthusiasm and can't contain myself. I transcribed interviews for a summer job once, and it gave me this big insight into how actual spoken words look like when pressed down onto the page. All the repetitions, stumbling, sentence fragments, little affirmative interjections; all of those lovely imperfections that remind you of real life. After that, I've felt that all the speech I've since read in written fiction is lacking – like an abstraction, a Platonic theoretical, an idealised life drawing without the wrinkles and asymmetries of an actual human body.</p>
<p>But, damn, you captured it. Perfectly. That third log in particular – I could actually hear these guys in my head, I could hear their pauses, their cadences, their inflections; and that never, ever happens to me, because written dialogue is nearly always smooshed into the mould of narrative expediency, or simplistic characterisation, or whatever, and comes out looking like weird jelly. But this dialogue breathes, and moves, and gesticulates. It's not a puppet – it's a real boy.</p>
<p>I'm taking notes from this. I'm analysing how you conveyed interrupted speech, how the characters self-corrected previous sentences with more precise near-repetitions, people talking over each other, the lot. You may recall me being pretty snotty about Cragglewood – and I don't recant those comments, unfortunately! – but I'm bringing it up just to say that I don't dole out praise of this magnitude easily. I hope you can be really proud of the dialogue you've written here. Because you should be.</p>
<hr />
<p>(Oh, one of the PUAs says <em>Cicada montana</em> instead of <em>Cicadetta montana</em>. Is that an intentional illustration that this brash dickwad is using powers beyond his ken without appreciating the details and consequences of doing so?)</p>

							</div>

								
					
