<p>Thanks! And I will refrain from reposting, if only to avoid the wrath of the Downvote Gods.</p>
<p>Also, thank you for the feedback! I made a few changes based on what you pointed out:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Changes</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>You're probably right about the cross-links, but I kind of wanted to be upfront about this, rather than cagey; I didn't want the reader to feel like the twist was that it was the Cicada God &#8212; I wanted the reader to know it was the Cicada God from Page 1, and for the twist to be that the PUAs and Tai Lopezes of the world are the Cicada God's blessed, Chosen Champions. That being said: I feel like if you don't click on those links, the piece works just fine.</li>
<li>The 'His Children' bit is something I thoughtlessly changed on a revision edit because I realized it was kind of incorrect (since he's speaking to a crowd), but you're absolutely right. On top of that, I now distinctly remember that the bit I stole this from (with Osteen) had him using the singular. So, changed!</li>
<li>Re: &quot;Coughing up cicadas&quot; &#8212; the PUAs are supposed to be anomalous, but I agree that it's a little&#8230; '?!?!'. It also feels like 'spoopy for spoopy's sake' to me, in rereading it. I've changed it to something more low-key (the women start chittering and hissing). I like this more because it also implies that by using it, you're possibly transforming women into SCP-3089-A instances, which is <em>way</em> more fucking horrible than making them vomit up cicadas.</li>
</ul>
<p>Finally, re: <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-3074">Kafka's Parking Garage</a> &#8212; I think part of it is that bugs terrify me, but part of it is also that in my mind, I see being transformed to a bug as being reduced to the lowest thing possible; you're just a few steps away from bacteria. To crudely steal from another religion's motif: You're at the bottom of the karmic food-chain. You're a fucking <em>insect</em>.</p>
<p>In a sense, that's kind of what I happening here: In their desperation to acquire various forms of success, the SCP-3089-A instances have sacrificed everything &#8212; their bodies, their minds, their <em>spirituality</em> &#8212; and have surrendered their identities up to a vast and terrible intelligence they don't comprehend.</p>
<p>This is pretty much what's happening in <a href="/scp-3074">SCP-3074</a>, too. The protagonist is so desperate to win the approval of this perfect stranger that they sacrifice everything about themselves &#8212; their independence, their will, their physical body, their very identity &#8212; it seems very logical to me that they would then be reduced to a chittering, scuttling insect.</p>
<p>I guess what I'm saying is that while I don't know if the two skips are <em>directly</em> related, the creatures at the bottom of Kafka's Parking Garage would definitely get along with instances of SCP-3090-A &#8212; and the voice on the phone probably has the Cicada God's number on speed-dial.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

